cartridge "DIGGER/COMPUTE!'S GAZETTE/1985"

' DIGGER
' A ColecoVision game based on "Digger", COMPUTE!'s Gazette, March 1985
' (disk 1985-03, Commodore 64 and VIC-20 machine language; no author named
' on the disk or in the program). Made for Amy Studio by Claude (an AI made
' by Anthropic), 2026, in Amy by Amy Bienvenu.
'
' The game: you dig tunnels through the earth under a hill. Three trolls
' dig after you. Boulders lie in the earth; dig away the earth under one
' and, after a moment of rumbling, it drops until it lands on earth again
' or on the bottom. A falling boulder squashes any troll it meets (10
' points). Squash all three trolls to clear the screen (100 points) and
' start the next one. A troll that touches you, or a boulder that drops on
' you, ends the game; so does the last boulder reaching the bottom while
' trolls are left (nothing can squash them any more).
'
' From the original (orig/dg.dis, the C64 machine code at $C000): one
' digger, three trolls, three boulders at fixed places every screen (the
' table at $C558, scaled from the C64's 296 x 133 pixel field to 224 x
' 144); the field starts full of earth and every creature erases earth as
' it moves ($C7F1); the digger keeps moving in the last direction pushed,
' only the four straight directions count ($C5C8, table $C5FD); a troll
' heads for the digger along x and y at once and only looks again after a
' random 2-33 of its turns ($C6E1, $C702, $C740); trolls dig too and pass
' through boulders; a boulder falls when the earth under its full width is
' gone, after a wait (50 passes, $C141), a pixel at a time, and stops on
' earth; a falling boulder kills the trolls it touches, 10 points each
' ($C176); no trolls left: 100 points and the next screen, the same layout
' ($C291); touching a troll or a boulder ends the game, as do all three
' boulders on the bottom ($C09A); the status lines SCORE, HI-SCORE, TROLLS,
' SCREENS ($C2BB); the C64's SHIFT pause.
'
' Liberties: the play loop runs 30 passes a second (the C64's ran about
' one creature turn every two frames); the digger moves 224/256 pixel a
' pass (26 pixels a second), a troll 96/256 on screen 1 (+8/256 a
' screen, at most 176/256), a boulder falls a pixel a pass after a 32-pass
' rumble (the C64 waited 50 of its passes); the trolls are slower against
' the digger than on the C64 (about 1.5 times slower there, 2.3 here on
' screen 1) and the rumble is shorter so that a person, reacting a quarter
' of a second late, can clear the first screens: a test player with that
' delay, luring trolls under boulders, cleared screen 1 in about 4 games
' out of 5 (with the first port's 116/256 and 38 passes: about 1 in 3);
' the earth is cut in 4 x 4 pixel cells
' (the C64 erased single pixels); a boulder at rest blocks the digger
' instead of killing him (on the C64 a touch was a sprite collision and
' killed); the game ends when every boulder is on the bottom (the C64
' needed all three there; with fewer it went on with no way to win, so
' the same thing); the left button stops the digger (the C64 digger could
' not stop); the right button pauses (SHIFT on the C64); the trolls wait two seconds
' before their first move (the C64's came at once); trolls get a
' little faster each screen (the C64 never changed); the game starts from
' a title page and ends with GAME OVER (the C64 restarted at once); earth
' in three colours, a miner with a lamp, trolls, boulders that shake,
' sounds and a title tune are new.
'
' Controls: joystick only. The stick sets the digging direction (up, down,
' left, right; diagonals are ignored), the left button stops, the right
' button pauses. On the title, up and down choose (START, HOW TO PLAY,
' CREDITS), a button picks; HOW TO PLAY has two pages (a button turns the
' page, 30 s idle goes back).
'
' House rules: text in columns 2-29, rows 1-22; tiles from code 128; the
' frame loop is protected from the NMI; screens drawn with the NMI off
' after a wait, display off. Music first in the sound table, effects after;
' the title tune is played once. `set sprite count` after every `clear
' sprites`. Own xorshift random numbers.

